    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 1: Newton maps N z 4 p ( z ) with an exceptional point"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 33,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 104.0, 477.0, 541.333, 507.667 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1. Let p be generic. If p is linear, then it follows from the Scaling property that N R ( z ) is conjugate to N z z − 1 ."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 34,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 117.667, 422.333, 541.667, 471.667 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Let p be non-linear. As p is generic and N z d p ( z ) is a polynomial, we have zp ′ ( z ) − dp ( z ) = α for some non-zero α . Letting y = p ( z ), we have the first-order linear differential equation y ′ − d z y = α z . The solution is"
  }, {
    "type" : "formula",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Formula",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 244.0, 388.0, 397.463, 414.0 ],
    "content" : "Q zd 1 2 dzd"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 118.0, 315.0, 541.667, 379.333 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "for an arbitrary constant β . Therefore, p ( z ) = βz d − α d and R ( z ) = z d βz d − α d . Consider c such that c d = α dβ and use the Scaling property to see that N R is conjugate to N βR ( cz ) . We are done since βR ( cz ) = z d z d − 1 and the resulting Newton map is z d +1 +( d − 1) z d ."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 37,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 103.333, 240.667, 542.333, 302.0 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2. Let n denote the number of distinct roots of p . Using the Scaling property, we assume without loss of generality that 1 is a multiple root of p whenever p is not generic. Along with this, what is going to be repeatedly used in all the following cases is that g ( z ) is a non-zero constant (see Equation (3.1))."
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.557, 115.313, 540.004, 232.143 ],
    "numbering style" : "english letters in lower case",
    "number of list items" : 2,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 124.207, 139.471, 540.004, 232.143 ],
      "font" : "CMR12",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "(a) Let deg( p ) = 3. Then there are three cases depending on the values of n . If n = 3, then p is generic, and from the first part of this theorem, it follows that p ( z ) = z 3 − 1, and hence N R ( z ) = 1 3 z ( z 3 +2). If n = 2, then p has a root with multiplicity 2 and therefore p ( z ) = ( z − 1) 2 ( z − a ), where a ̸ = 0 , 1. In this case, g ( z ) = − ( a + 2) z + 3 a and therefore a = − 2. Thus, N R ( z ) = z 6 ( z 2 + z + 4). If n = 1, then p ( z ) = ( z − 1) 3 , and we get N R ( z ) = 1 3 z ( z + 2).",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 123.557, 115.313, 540.004, 141.725 ],
      "font" : "CMR12",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "(b) Let deg( p ) = 4. All possible cases of p and the resulting Newton maps are given in Table 1.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
